## 4. Content structure for Google and AI visibility

Google's official position, confirmed in the May 15, 2026 AI guide: there is no special markup or generative engine trick. AI Overviews and AI Mode are built from the regular index, and standard SEO with genuinely helpful content is the path in. Google explicitly lists llms.txt, content chunking, and AI specific rewriting as things you do not need. At the same time, AI Overviews appear at large scale and cut organic click through roughly in half on affected queries, and citation studies reward the same structural habits. The competitive objective has shifted from ranking first to being the source the AI answer cites. The structural work that earns that is also just good SEO:

- H2 and H3 headers written as the question the user asked. "What services does [Brand] offer?" not "Our Services".
- The first one to two sentences after each header answer the question directly. Answer first, elaborate after. Answer first extraction is what AI surfaces quote, and the strongest pattern is a two to four sentence direct answer to the page's core question placed above the fold.
- An FAQ section on every service page, five to seven questions matching "People Also Ask" intent, answers two to four sentences. The visible content is the value. The FAQPage markup is optional since rich results ended.
- Definitions for key terms, which AI systems pull.
- Bullet lists and numbered steps for process content.
- Internal linking: every service page links to related pages, blog posts link to relevant service pages.
- Original first hand insight. A page with first hand data, a real case study, or a specific practitioner conclusion gives an AI answer something to cite with confidence. Generic roundups rarely earn citations regardless of ranking.
- NAP and brand description consistent across the site and major profiles. Brand mentions across the web correlate with AI citation more strongly than backlinks do.
- Visible updated dates on content pages. Freshness is weighted heavily by Perplexity and ChatGPT search.
- Descriptive image file names, never "IMG_1234".

**AI crawler access, audit and do not change:**

- robots.txt must not block retrieval bots if AI visibility is wanted: OAI-SearchBot and ChatGPT-User (OpenAI), Claude-SearchBot and Claude-User (Anthropic), PerplexityBot and Perplexity-User. Training bots (GPTBot, ClaudeBot, Google-Extended) are a separate client policy decision, and blocking Google-Extended does not affect Google Search rankings.
- Check the CDN and WAF layer (Vercel firewall, Cloudflare bot rules). A large share of sites block AI crawlers there by accident. Flag any challenge or block rule hitting the agents above.
- Confirm the site is indexable in Bing, since ChatGPT search still leans on Bing's index. Flag if Bing Webmaster Tools is not set up.
- llms.txt: not recommended. Google says to ignore it and major crawlers do not honor it. Flag time spent on it as wasted, with the one exception of developer documentation sites.

**Measurement note for the report:** AI Overviews and AI Mode are folded into overall Search traffic in Search Console. Recommend the client segment by query type and watch for the pattern of impressions rising while click through falls, which is the signature of an AI Overview intercepting clicks above their result.

